Z||000000||Blade||08-28-2000||11:39 AM||||Ok i give whats the trick to outside key part? I got the old one out and tried getting the new one in...pushed the clp back in place(I thought). Lock wasnt held though i screwd with it till i lost the damn clip now i need to go to junk yard and try to get another. So what is the trick to get them cliped? Pull the plastic part of the door and get it from that side?<P>Blade||||reg||1
Z||000001||HugGRRRFiero||08-28-2000||12:37 PM||||  That door lock cylinder clip is a tricky bugger. <P>I replaced mine when the door was off the car and I could lay it down flat which I think made it easier, but I'm not recommending that you take the door off to do it.  <P>I used long needle nose pliers, a long flat screwdriver.  Use the pliers to hold the tab and steady the clip.  Then use the screwdriver to turn, pry and push the clip on.  If you can have a friend press and hold the lock cylinder in place.  Hope this helps...<P>Brent<P>------------------<BR>    <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/huggrrr.gif">  <BR>  <A HREF="mailto:HugGRRRFiero@hotmail.com">HugGRRRFiero@hotmail.com</A> ||||reg||1
Z||000002||Blade||08-28-2000||01:34 PM||||I tried that and then i wiggled the lock and it slid back into the door  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/frown.gif">. so im figuring it wasnt cliped in right place. Now the clip is lost and i gota get nother one  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/frown.gif"><P>Blade||||reg||1
Z||000003||HugGRRRFiero||08-28-2000||02:57 PM||||   I see, you might want to see if you can get that lost clip out using a magnet, maybe it will come out.  That's if it's not wedged in there good.<P>Taking the door skin off would be a big job just to get that little clip out.  You should be able to clip the lock cylinder from inside the door.  I remember it took me a couple tries, I had to press the cylinder in pretty good for the clip to find the tabs.<p>[This message has been edited by HugGRRRFiero (edited 08-28-2000).]||||reg||1
Z||000004||Blade||08-28-2000||05:01 PM||||it was moving all the way towards the back of the car(as far as it was suposed to slide). But it seemed to not be in the slots, how you get it to press against the metal ie not towards the skin. After id slide it over id wiggle the key and lock would come loose.<BR>It sucked.<P>Blade||||reg||1
Z||000005||HugGRRRFiero||08-28-2000||05:31 PM||||  I used the pliers to hold the clip up against the metal backing and tipped it just enough so I knew the fingers of the clip would be up against the metal.<P>Once the lock cylinder was in place I just turned the screwdriver against the gap between the backside of the clip tab and crash beam to get leverage to drive the clip home.  It's just something that takes three hands and a few tries to get it, takes some patience too.  It's not a well though out design...  but it worked.||||reg||1
Z||000006||Monkeyman||08-28-2000||07:25 PM||||Not a bad design when everything was brand new, but now that our cars are at least 12 years old (with bad dew wipes, letting dirt, etc in) it's a pain.  Also, I think that the C-clips get more of a spring with age.  I got a new one (still in the package) when I lost one of my old ones (still in the door) and it didn't have as much curvature to it.||||reg||1
